---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ProductSpec from '@/components/ProductSpec.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Hammerstein.ai | Ray Weiss"
  description="Strategic-reasoning AI for tabletop wargames. Paste your board state, get back Auftragstaktik orders. $15 / month."
>
  <main class="doc">
    <div class="doc__frame">
      <header class="page-heading">
        <h1>Hammerstein.ai</h1>
        <p class="dek">
          Strategic-reasoning AI for tabletop wargames, built around the Hammerstein-Equord
          doctrine.
        </p>
        <p class="dek">
          Paste your board state, a status report, and the turn question. Get back Auftragstaktik
          orders.
        </p>
      </header>

      <ProductSpec>
        <div class="infra-meta">
          <span
            ><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v"
              >Live &middot; early access</span
            ></span
          >
          <span><span class="k">Price</span><span class="v">$15 / month</span></span>
          <span><span class="k">Limit</span><span class="v">120 turns / month</span></span>
          <span
            ><span class="k">Demo</span><span class="v">5 turns / IP / day &middot; free</span
            ></span
          >
        </div>
        <p class="spec-note">
          Live: <a href="https://hammerstein.ai">hammerstein.ai</a>. Benchmark: <a
            href="https://hammerstein.ai/benchmark/">hammerstein.ai/benchmark</a
          >. Journal: <a href="https://hammerstein.ai/journal/">hammerstein.ai/journal</a>. The
          framework underneath: <a href="/hammerstein">the CLI</a> &middot; <a
            href="/hammerstein-model">the 7B model</a
          >.
        </p>
      </ProductSpec>

      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2>What it does</h2>

        <p>
          Wargamer mode is the live surface. Paste your board state, a short status report, and the
          turn question you're trying to decide. The model returns kriegspiel-style Auftragstaktik
          orders &mdash; situation, intent, main effort, supporting effort, end-state &mdash; for
          the side you're playing. Same framework as <a href="/hammerstein">the open-source CLI</a>,
          applied to wargame turns instead of plan audits.
        </p>

        <h2>What's shipped</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Text-input turns</strong> &mdash; board state + status + question &rarr; Auftragstaktik
            orders. Sonnet 4.6 wrapped in the Hammerstein system prompt. The primary surface.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Board-photo upload</strong> &mdash; JPG or PNG resized to 1600px in your browser with
            EXIF rotation preserved, attached as a multimodal reference image per turn.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Rulebook ingest</strong> &mdash; paste rules text or drop a PDF; parsed client-side
            via pdf.js, never uploaded. Cited when a specific rule shapes the order.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Persistent campaign memory across machines</strong> &mdash; paid-tier campaigns sync
            server-side via Cloudflare D1. Start a campaign on your laptop, resume on your phone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Anti-metagame ethical constraint</strong> &mdash; the model does not card-count or
            game hidden information; the constraint is a hard rule in the system prompt, not a courtesy.
            Challenge mode toggles it off when you want the AI playing without that restraint.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Benchmark</h2>

        <p>
          On the framework-discipline benchmark, the system prompt wins across model scales &mdash;
          frontier wraps (Opus 4.7 / Sonnet 4.6 / GPT-5), out-of-domain question sets, and a 7B
          distilled local model. v0.7 human-judge results compiling. Methodology, per-generation
          results, and the replication kit live at
          <a href="https://hammerstein.ai/benchmark/">hammerstein.ai/benchmark</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Pricing</h2>

        <p>
          $15 / month for 120 turns &mdash; sized for a real campaign cadence, not metered by the
          day. First 50 subscribers; pricing rises after. The free demo runs at 5 turns per IP per
          day for anyone who wants to test the surface before subscribing.
        </p>

        <h2>Try it</h2>

        <p>
          Live at <a href="https://hammerstein.ai">hammerstein.ai</a>. The free demo doesn't require
          a subscription &mdash; just paste a turn and see what comes back. The subscriber tier
          unlocks the 120-turn-per-month cap and cross-machine persistent campaign memory.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
